  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Cam Schlittler dominated with eleven strikeouts, but the offense couldn't finish what the pitching gave them.",
    "what_this_means": "A one-run game tied through nine innings, decided in the tenth by a team that never led. Schlittler's 7.0 innings of surgical control. Three hits, one run, eleven strikeouts. Gave the Yankees a real chance to win this. They didn't. Two runners on base in the sixth inning, two outs. Nothing. Grisham tied it at 1 in the eighth with a solo shot, the only swing that landed. After that, silence. The bullpen held clean. Castro and Blackburn combining for three scoreless frames. But clean relief doesn't matter when the lineup goes 4-for-27 with nine strikeouts. This is what pitching excellence looks like when the offense starves it. A starter pitches the game of his life and still loses because four hits and one run is not a victory condition.",
    "what_to_watch": "They had the window open for nine innings. Window's closed now. This team doesn't turn one-run leads into wins by hoping. They turn them by manufacturing runs in the sixth or seventh when chances exist. They didn't. That's not a slump. That's a closed door.",
